🔎 Deep Dive Q&A with Examples
Q1: What is the difference between Value Type and Reference Type in C#?
Answer:

Value Type: Stores data directly in memory (stack). Examples: int, struct.

Reference Type: Stores a reference (pointer) to data (heap). Examples: class, string, object.

Example:

csharp
int a = 10;       // Value type
int b = a;        // Copy of value
b = 20;           // 'a' remains 10

class Person { public string Name; }
Person p1 = new Person();
p1.Name = "Manoranjan";
Person p2 = p1;   // Reference copy
p2.Name = "Broo"; // p1.Name also changes
👉 Interview Tip: Emphasize stack vs heap and copy vs reference behavior.

Q2: Explain Encapsulation with an example.
Answer:  
Encapsulation is wrapping data and methods into a single unit (class) and restricting direct access using access modifiers.

Example:

csharp
class BankAccount {
    private double balance; // hidden data

    public void Deposit(double amount) {
        balance += amount;
    }

    public double GetBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}
👉 Interview Tip: Show how private protects data and public exposes controlled access.

Q3: What is the difference between Method Overloading and Method Overriding?
Answer:

Overloading: Same method name, different parameters (compile-time polymorphism).

Overriding: Redefining a base class method in a derived class (runtime polymorphism).

Example:

csharp
// Overloading
class Calculator {
    public int Add(int a, int b) => a + b;
    public double Add(double a, double b) => a + b;
}

// Overriding
class Animal {
    public virtual void Speak() => Console.WriteLine("Animal sound");
}
class Dog : Animal {
    public override void Speak() => Console.WriteLine("Woof!");
}
Q4: What is the difference between Abstract Class and Interface?
Answer:

Abstract Class: Can have fields, constructors, and implemented methods. Supports inheritance.

Interface: Only contains method signatures (until C# 8 default implementations). Supports multiple inheritance.

Example:

csharp
abstract class Shape {
    public abstract void Draw();
    public void Info() => Console.WriteLine("Shape Info");
}

interface IPrintable {
    void Print();
}

class Circle : Shape, IPrintable {
    public override void Draw() => Console.WriteLine("Drawing Circle");
    public void Print() => Console.WriteLine("Printing Circle");
}
Q5: Explain Delegates and Events in C#.
Answer:

Delegate: Type-safe function pointer.

Event: Wrapper around delegate, used for publish-subscribe model.

Example:

csharp
public delegate void Notify(); // delegate

class Process {
    public event Notify ProcessCompleted; // event

    public void Start() {
        Console.WriteLine("Process Started");
        ProcessCompleted?.Invoke(); // trigger event
    }
}

class Program {
    static void Main() {
        Process p = new Process();
        p.ProcessCompleted += () => Console.WriteLine("Process Finished!");
        p.Start();
    }
}
Q6: Explain LINQ with an example.
Answer:  
LINQ (Language Integrated Query) allows querying collections using SQL-like syntax.

Example:

csharp
int[] numbers = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 };

var evenNumbers = from n in numbers
                  where n % 2 == 0
                  select n;

foreach (var num in evenNumbers)
    Console.WriteLine(num); // 2,4,6
Q7: What is Dependency Injection (DI) in C#?
Answer:  
DI is a design pattern where dependencies are injected into a class instead of being created inside it. Improves testability and loose coupling.

Example:

csharp
interface IMessageService {
    void SendMessage(string msg);
}

class EmailService : IMessageService {
    public void SendMessage(string msg) => Console.WriteLine("Email: " + msg);
}

class Notification {
    private readonly IMessageService _service;
    public Notification(IMessageService service) {
        _service = service;
    }
    public void Notify(string msg) => _service.SendMessage(msg);
}

class Program {
    static void Main() {
        IMessageService service = new EmailService();
        Notification n = new Notification(service);
        n.Notify("Hello DI!");
    }
